{
 "id": "2026-cirella-urban",
 "name": "Urban Rewilding Scenarios - Design Option Choice",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: residents choose how far a public space should be rewilded. Two places in Sopot, Poland (a pedestrian bridge between buildings in a park, and the central plaza) are shown in random order. For each place, three photomontage design options from the paper's Figure 1 appear side by side: signature landscape (\"minimal rewilding\"), moderate rewilding and intensive rewilding, with the paper's one-line description of each. The participant picks the preferred option; age, gender and whether they live in Sopot are asked at the end. Original study: 1,000 Sopot residents, face-to-face with poster boards; moderate rewilding was preferred in both places (bridge 58.7%, plaza 47.9%), and intensive rewilding was chosen more often at the plaza (41.4%) than at the bridge (27.0%). Analysis: share of each option per place and a chi-square test of option x place. The figure is CC BY-NC-ND 4.0, so it is shown unaltered and each panel is a cropped view of the same file. Differences from the original study: (1) online on a screen instead of face-to-face poster boards with an enumerator who could clarify; (2) scenario order is random instead of alternated between respondents; (3) the question wording and the demographic questions were written for this template (the paper does not print them), and age is asked as a number instead of quota groups; (4) non-residents are not screened out, they answer the residence question instead; (5) the paper's expert survey of 32 municipal officials is not included.",
